A: I'd recommend starting with the Practical Code Contracts on Code Contracts official website, particularly on this page that explains how to create a test method for an ArrayList contract.

Practical Code Contracts is a set of practical examples showing how to apply Code Contracts to practice problems in various domains. It includes the following samples:

- ArrayListContractTest: Contains examples used to test the contracts of the ArrayList, Dictionary, and Queue classes.

Understand that PEX is part of Pex/Playback from Microsoft Research and this is a tool used for automated testing. It can generate a lot of test cases for your program, but it doesn't guarantee a 100% coverage or a high quality of the tests it produces, it's more of a tool for generating a large amount of test cases based on the provided preconditions and invariants.

To use Pex, you have to annotate your methods with the [PexMethod] attribute and any parameters you are interested in for the test generation with the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute. Then you can run the Pex test directly, it will generate a large quantity of tests for your method.

I recommend you to get familiar with some manual testing as well, to make sure that your methods are behaving as expected. Automated tests are a good way of validating these behaviours, you can have thousands of automated tests, but even a small part of them can help you to identify a regression or some other kind of error.

Additionally, I could recommend to you to read some articles about best practices of software testing, and about how to use Pex:

- Test Code: Automated Unit Testing
- Practical Code Contracts
- Microsoft Research: Practical Code Contracts
- Pex/Playback: Practical Automated Theorem Proving
- Pex: Practical Automated Testing: A Comprehensive Guide to Practice Problem Solutions

These articles from Microsoft Research will help you understand how to use Pex, how to create your tests, how to interpret the results, etc. 

Also, you may consider using FsCheck: a QuickCheck-inspired property-based testing tool for .NET languages (C# or VB.Net), which is an automated testing tool, that generates random input data to test your properties. It's more focused on properties so you could have a comprehensive test coverage. It's available in Nuget.

Remember that software testing is not just about checking your code manually but the whole process, where you start with requirements, then design and create your test scenarios, code your test cases, run your test, check the results manually for bugs or errors and, finally, automate this process by creating your test cases.
Hope this will help you get started on your testing journey.

