A: The Pex framework is only designed to test contracts on method implementations provided in the code under unit test. It does not infer the full code under test's functionality. What you may be observing here is some aspect of the test that does not behave as expected. That said, you have written your test assuming the object is the same as in the original test run, which is not true. The test is indeed checking index of an element in the list, but it's using a different (possibly mutated) list as a part of a larger test that should be run in isolation.
When you change the method under test from PexInstrumentedSolution.Program.IndexOfTest to MyMethod, the test starts treating MyMethod's return values as the ones that need to be checked. 

Your understanding is correct as IndexOf test fails. It checks for negative values so you won't be able to see correct indices of elements which would fail the test.
As to why the test is marked as passed, it's because the last assert checks for the "negative" scenario for nonexistent elements in the list and only this one assertion fails, while asserting equality on the element's index in the array is probably a precondition to that element being in array.
In your case, the unit test is passing because it's failing only for an assertion about non-existence of an item in array. If Item.x was in the ArrayList arrList before you added another x, the ArrayList's IndexOf should return non-negative index not equal to -1, and asserts will fail. If index of Item.x remained constant, and you have added another x item, IndexOf for Item.x should not return same index as before, and this assertion should catch it.
It's possible you didn't add any elements to your ArrayList before Pex run, and when you manually executed your test, nothing was in the list, so your test was passing. After Pex run, it added elements to the list, and your assertion is checking for.
You could check if the object you are testing was successfully added to the ArrayList by observing the changes after the call to Add in Pex method under test. 
Try adding [PexMethodUnderTest] attribute to your test to have Pex treat the method implementation as the one which is actually under test.
Also make sure that Pex can indeed find your method with your attributes and also with the appropriate parameters and return type.

