The full example source can be found here: https://github.com/DotNetAnalyzers/Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.FxCopAnalyzers/tree/master/test/Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Test.FxCopAnalyzers/UnitTests/TestCases/ArrayListContractTest.cs
Notice that we're making assumptions about the precondition, while also observing values for later assertions. If the PUT operation was valid, you should be able to deduce from the observed values the behavior of the operation. This way, you can add your assertions based on the assumptions you've made. 

The method IndexOfContract is tested for a list "arrList" and value "x", if "x" is in the list "arrList", its index is retrieved and if "x" was added to the list "arrList", the Count of the list should increase by one.

This provides us with the ability to not only verify the postcondition of an operation, but also verify the precondition of an operation. A PUT operation in this example is adding an element to a list. 

By creating these assertions, you can check the behavior of the program without running it and you get the information you need to understand the behavior of the operation. It can be used to test ArrayList.Add method and ensure its pre and post conditions.

Remember that the purpose of Code Contracts is to provide a way to express a contract with respect to invariants that your code is supposed to maintain. When you specify your contract, Pex will generate tests that stress test your contract and find bugs.

Code contracts are about writing code that is understandable, predictable, and maintainable. When you are writing code contracts, you are saying something about your code and you are promising that you will never break that promise. The ultimate goal of code contracts is not to catch bugs – instead, it is to ensure that your code will behave in expected ways.

Here, as per Contract-Based Testing of Code, we’ve seen that for PUT, ArrayList.Add, the test checks if x is added to the list and its count has increased. Using Contracts, we are able to verify the pre and post conditions.

However, it’s important to mention that contract testing may not cover all the scenarios and it’s highly suggested to perform stress tests or regression tests due to the limitations of contract testing. Contract Testing is about checking your contracts and not checking everything.
Another point is, if you are working in a team environment, each member should have a coverage of their code by automated testing so that unexpected bugs do not slip in.

Here's an additional resources on Contract testing:

- Microsoft's code contract testing github: https://github.com/Microsoft/CodeContracts
- More details on Code Contracts: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd264995.aspx
- Practical Code Contracts: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-code-coverage-to-determine-how-much-code-is-being-tested

Hope this helps you understand contract testing. It’s a quite effective tool when you can provide strong contract for your functionality.

P.S - Thanks to Pex team for providing an example and clarifications regarding this topic.

