A: The array list contract does not require the use of any method, including LastIndexOf(), to be consistent with its implementation.  It merely describes the behavior of the collection contract for the methods you have selected.  So, any behavioral discrepancy between your implementation and the contract violations are not an error.  Instead, you are supposed to explain any differences in behavior to the Pex engine to help it generate more appropriate test cases.  

So in your case, you should try to explain the difference in behavior to Pex engine. If the LastIndexOf method is not implemented correctly, you can show this by writing a precondition stating that LastIndexOf and Contains methods should return the same values. For example:
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexHasNoOutgoingCalls]
public int WrappedLastIndexOf([PexAssumeNotNull]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeValid] int value)
{
    int lastIndex = list.LastIndexOf(value);
    bool containsValue = list.Contains(value);
    PexAssume.IsTrue(lastIndex == -1 || (0 <= lastIndex && lastIndex < list.Count));
    PexAssume.IsTrue(containsValue == (lastIndex != -1));
    return lastIndex;
}

Pex will now understand that LastIndexOf and Contains should return the same values and it will generate more appropriate inputs for tests. 

Additionally, it might be helpful to reevaluate your selection and contract because it seems like you've selected a small set of methods, not the whole contract for ArrayList. 

For a contract which describes the properties and behavior of objects (including your ArrayList), you might want to include more methods in your selection and also include contract assertions that verify them. In case some behavior is missing, you can always explain that to the Pex engine.

Note: I would also recommend you to use Generics instead of using Boxing and ArrayList, as you can get more benefits with Generics.

A: Yes, LastIndexOf method does not comply with the ArrayList contract, as it does not throw an exception if the item is not found but instead returns -1, which is an indicator that the item was not found. Therefore, any tests for LastIndexOf should explain the expected behavior to Pex. In this case, you should write a precondition stating that if LastIndexOf returns a non-negative number that number should be equal to the number of elements in the list minus one because index in C# is zero based. 

public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeNotNull]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid] int x)
{
    int ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool exists = arrList.Contains(x);
    PexAssume.IsTrue(ret == -1 || (0 <= ret && ret <= arrList.Count - 1));
    PexAssume.IsTrue(exists == (ret != -1));
}

The Pex engine should be able to handle the difference in behavior now and generate more appropriate test cases. Note that, in general, when describing behavior, it is usually important to specify the expected result and the "real" result as you might have different expectations about the expected result.

Hope this helps.

A: From Microsoft's Documentation and from Pex's perspective, LastIndexOf() method violates the ArrayList contract since it does not imply that LastIndexOf() does the opposite of IndexOf(). And since the ArrayList contract requires that IndexOf() matches the last occurrence of an item, LastIndexOf() should also match the last occurrence, not just any occurrence at all.
So, I would suggest you to remove LastIndexOf() from--------------------